\section*{FPRAS for common matroid bases}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents the following claims relevant to this question, with manuscript numbers following paper order in Sources. In manuscript 1 (family 114; 2026-09-23), the asserted result is that two arbitrary equal-rank matroids supplied by independence oracles admit an FPRAS for their common bases, with polynomial work on every execution.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Manuscript 1.}
For arbitrary rank-$r$ matroids $M_1,M_2$ on $[n]$, supplied only by independence oracles, let $Z$ count subsets that are bases of both. Given rational $\varepsilon,\delta\in(0,1)$, the claimed uniform oracle algorithm returns $\widehat Z\in\mathbb Q_{\ge0}$ with
\[
 \Pr[(1-\varepsilon)Z\le\widehat Z\le(1+\varepsilon)Z]\ge1-\delta.
\]
When there is no common base it always outputs zero. One polynomial bounds both the number of oracle queries and the bit time, on all executions, as a function of $n$, encoding length $\ell$ of $n,r,\varepsilon,\delta$, $\varepsilon^{-1}$ and $\log\delta^{-1}$. No linear representation or special matroid class is assumed.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 114; 2026-09-23), a paired-ground-set construction represents common bases as transversals of an auxiliary matroid. Quadratic signature inequalities compare defect classes and yield a transport bound, after which annealing learns multipliers and warm restarts estimate the partition ratios.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The result supplies the conclusion in this problem with its displayed hypotheses and quantifiers. The broader statements and applications in the manuscript are kept separate from the particular question above. The supporting result is manuscript 1 (family 114; 2026-09-23).

\subsection{Formal verification}
For manuscript 1 (family 114; 2026-09-23), OpenAI lists Lean statements for this assertion: two arbitrary equal-rank matroids supplied by independence oracles admit an FPRAS for their common bases, with polynomial work on every execution.

The selected statement includes the clause used for this problem.

The linked family Comparator checks are \texttt{CommonBasesFPRAS.lean}.

Their named declarations include \texttt{OAI.common\_bases\_fpras}.

This site has not executed the Lean code. Described statement coverage is not an independent proof check.

\subsection{Status caveat}
The manuscripts were produced by an internal OpenAI model. OpenAI cautions that results without formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently checked the proofs; the label records the scope of OpenAI's claim.

\subsection{Sources}
